    <figure class="splash" id="Figure02_00_Justice">
      <media alt="A photo of a statue depicting the figure of Justice.">
        <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure02_00_Justice.jpg"/>
      </media>
    <caption>
      The Figure of Justice. Their accuracy and practical use in the marketplace made scales, held aloft here by the figure of Justice in Bruges, Belgium, a common symbol in jurisprudence and law in the East and the West. Even today, the concept of counterbalancing different ideas and philosophies underlies many approaches to the law and ethics. (credit: modification of “Golden Lady Justice” by Emmanuel Huybrechts/Flickr, CC BY 2.0)
      </caption>
    </figure>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>[H2]Introduction</title>
    <para id="para-00001">
      Madeline and her wife Aliyah are awaiting the arrival of their daughter, Joyce. As adoptive parents-to-be they are nervous about what to expect. Aliyah is worried that their newborn daughter will miss her biological parents, or that she will show separation anxiety from them in the first few weeks of life. Madeline is not so sure, she thought infants did not even have emotions or feelings until they are a few months old. 
    </para>
    <para id="para-00002">
      Both mothers are curious as to how much influence they will have over Joyce’s personality. Madeline is concerned that biology will play a larger role and they may struggle to understand Joyce’s individuality. Aliyah is a musician and hopes that she can inspire Joyce to love music as much as her.
    </para>
    <para id="para-00003">
      But their biggest concern is that Joyce will have a happy childhood, will feel loved, and will form a loving bond with her moms. Both Madeline and Aliyah want her to feel like she belongs and help her to form a positive identity. They attended a parenting class and learned about infant attachment and hope that she will be able to form a secure stable relationship with both of them.
To get ready for their new arrival, Madeline is planning to take parental leave and Aliyah is researching reputable childcare centers for when her wife needs to return to work. They are also reaching out to family and friends for help with day-to-day tasks while they adjust to their new family member. Aliyah’s dad is excited to teach his granddaughter about his cultural traditions such as using cloth diapers and co-sleeping, whereas Madeline’s parents are looking forward to sharing their strategies for potty training.
    </para>
    <para id="para-00004">
      In this chapter, we will discuss the behavioral and emotional traits that infants display shortly after birth, how they learn from their caregivers to develop social and emotional skills, a sense of identity, and how the bond between caregivers and infants changes throughout the early years of our lives. By the end of this chapter, you will be able to advise families like Madeline and Aliyah’s on what to expect as they await their newest family member. WW-test notes that opener is 362 words.
    </para>
    </section>
  </content>
